Q: How can I implement transactional integrity across WordPress hooks when custom database tables are involved? A: <p>Yes, you can wrap it — $wpdb uses one connection for the whole request, so START TRANSACTION / ROLLBACK cover WooCommerce's queries too. Three things break this in practice, and none of them throw an error when they do.</p>
<p>Storage engine: rollback on a MyISAM table silently does nothing. Core is InnoDB on any recent install, but custom tables made by older plugins often are not. Check SHOW TABLE STATUS before you trust anything.</p>
<p>Implicit commits: any DDL in the same request commits your open transaction where it stands. A plugin running dbDelta() on init during a version check, or a CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE in someone's reporting query, ends your transaction early and you get a partial commit with no warning.</p>
<p>Side effects are not transactional. wc_create_order() is not just rows: it primes the object cache, fires woocommerce_new_order, which sends mail, schedules Action Scheduler jobs and queues webhooks. ROLLBACK removes the rows and leaves all of that. The cache is the mean one — after the rollback the order object can still sit in the object cache, so a later read sees an order that no longer exists in the database.</p>
<p>What holds up in practice:</p>
<pre><code>global $wpdb;
$wpdb-&gt;query('START TRANSACTION');
try {
    $id    = $this-&gt;insert_learner($user_id, $course_id);
    $order = wc_create_order([...]);
    $this-&gt;bump_stats($course_id);
    $wpdb-&gt;query('COMMIT');
} catch (\Throwable $e) {
    $wpdb-&gt;query('ROLLBACK');
    wp_cache_flush();
    return new WP_Error('enroll_failed', $e-&gt;getMessage(), ['status' =&gt; 500]);
}
do_action('myplugin_learner_enrolled', $id, $order-&gt;get_id());
</code></pre>
<p>Two rules behind that. All writes live in one method instead of being scattered across hooks — you cannot roll back what a hook did three files away. And your own do_action goes after the commit, so listeners that mail people or call an API never fire for a transaction that did not happen. If a listener must be part of the same unit of work, it is not a listener, it is a step in the method.</p>
<p>On the REST layer, take an idempotency key from the client and put a unique index on it. Clients retry timeouts, and a retry landing after a slow-but-successful first call will happily create a second order, transaction or not.</p>
<p>One caveat: MySQL has no nested transactions. If another plugin opens its own START TRANSACTION inside your request, its COMMIT closes yours. SAVEPOINT / ROLLBACK TO is the escape hatch, but needing it usually means the work should not be sitting inside a hook that third parties run code in.</p>

Q: How to update or add fields to a sublist item (array?) A: <p>In case this hasn't been resolved:</p>
<p><em>You are missing the payment_gateways layer</em>*:</p>
<p><code>payment_options</code> should be an object with property <code>payment_gateways</code> , as the <code>List</code></p>
<p>Zoho's error responses aren't very informative and you have to take some of them with a grain of salt.</p>
<p>When I see an error like</p>
<pre><code>{&quot;code&quot;:1038,&quot;message&quot;:&quot;JSON is not well formed&quot;}
</code></pre>
<p>I interpret it as, <em>the JSON does not match what they are <strong>expecting</strong></em>. Looking at your payload,  you lost the <code>payment_gateways</code> array (List in Deluge).</p>
<p>From your info statement:</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;expiry_date&quot;:&quot;2024-11-25&quot;,
    &quot;Amount&quot;:&quot;407.0&quot;,
    &quot;accept_retainer&quot;:true,
    &quot;retainer_percentage&quot;:&quot;25&quot;,
    &quot;configured&quot;:true,
    &quot;PaybyCC&quot;:true,
    &quot;payment_options&quot;:[
        {
            &quot;configured&quot;:true,
            &quot;can_show_billing_address&quot;:false,
            &quot;is_bank_account_applicable&quot;:false,
            &quot;can_pay_using_new_card&quot;:true,
            &quot;gateway_name&quot;:&quot;stripe&quot;
        }
    ]
}
</code></pre>
<p>When it should be:</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;expiry_date&quot;:&quot;2024-11-25&quot;,
    &quot;Amount&quot;:&quot;407.0&quot;,
    &quot;accept_retainer&quot;:true,
    &quot;retainer_percentage&quot;:&quot;25&quot;,
    &quot;configured&quot;:true,
    &quot;PaybyCC&quot;:true,
    &quot;payment_options&quot;:{
        &quot;payment_gateways&quot;: [
             {
                &quot;configured&quot;:true,
                &quot;can_show_billing_address&quot;:false,
                &quot;is_bank_account_applicable&quot;:false,
                &quot;can_pay_using_new_card&quot;:true,
                &quot;gateway_name&quot;:&quot;stripe&quot;
            }
        ]
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Deluge is not very friendly with building Maps and Lists; a good rule of thumb is to declare all your objects first and build from the inside out:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>quote = Map();
payment_options = Map();
payment_gateways = List();
payment_gateway = Map();

// don't include the newlines like I have below
// this is just for readability in the thread.
payment_gateway.putAll({
    &quot;configured&quot;: true, 
    &quot;can_show_billing_address&quot;: false, 
    &quot;is_bank_account_applicable&quot;: false, 
    &quot;can_pay_using_new_card&quot;: true, 
    &quot;gateway_name&quot;: &quot;stripe&quot; 
});

payment_gateways.add(payment_gateway);
payment_options.put(&quot;payment_gateways&quot;, payment_gateways);
quote.put(&quot;payment_options&quot;, payment_options);
</code></pre>

Q: JUnit Test cases failing after upgrading to Gradle 9, Springboot 4 and Java 25 A: <p>I was able to resolve the issue by adding the below under my <code>dependencyManagement</code> section</p>
<pre><code>dependencyManagement {
    applyMavenExclusions = false  &lt;-- this fixed it
    imports {
        mavenBom &quot;org.springframework.cloud:spring-cloud-dependencies:2025.1.2&quot;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Sharing here if this could be of help to someone who faces the same error</p>

Q: NS_ERROR_CORRUPTED_CONTENT in Live Server with TypeScript-generated JS files A: <p>It was an issue with the compiler. When importing, the extension has been removed, so I had to explicitly use <code>.js</code> and make <code>moduleResolution</code> to be a node in the <code>tsconfig.json</code> file:</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;compilerOptions&quot;: {
    &quot;moduleResolution&quot;: &quot;node&quot;,
    // other options...
  }
}
</code></pre>
